    "created_at": "2026-11-02T15:54:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-02T15:54:45Z",
    "body": "I'm investigating a standalone, source-free same-document navigation experiment, not a production framework. I noticed the intercepted-traverse race discussed here and would appreciate clarification from implementers/spec maintainers:\n\n1. In current Chromium, is an intercepted same-document `traverse` guaranteed not to resume after a later navigation has replaced its ongoing `NavigateEvent`, or is this still a known potential race between deferred traversal steps and supersession?\n2. If a user presses browser toolbar Back while a SPA has unsaved in-memory state, what invariants, if any, can a `navigate` listener rely on to distinguish (a) preventing the initial traversal before commitment, (b) applying a synchronous cancellation, (c) a navigation committed before app restoration, and (d) an unexpectedly aborted/superseded operation? In particular, are `cancelable`, `canIntercept`, and any available navigation identity sufficient, or are there cases where the application must report an unrecoverable/unsupported state?\n3. Are there existing web-platform tests or Chromium tests covering repeated toolbar Back during interception/compensating restoration, including history-action activation and competing navigation? Links would be especially helpful.\n\nI understand that no API can absolutely prevent cross-document departures or browser closure, and that finite passing tests cannot prove absence of races. This question is about *same-document* navigation safety and specification/implementation guarantees, not permission to trap a user in the page. I am not asking for a code review or product approval here.",
